WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.800
For years, running React Native on iOS meant relying on an engine that behaves like a heavy freight train.

2
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.000
It excels at maintaining speed over a long haul, but it struggles to build momentum from a complete stop.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.800
That engine is JavaScript Core, Apple's proprietary JavaScript runtime.

4
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:24.600
It is embedded deeply within iOS and served as the default tool to parse and execute React Native bundles.

5
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.600
This timeline illustrates the workload JavaScript Core was built for.

6
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:33.600
Web browsers, it uses jit compilation to optimize long running sessions.

7
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.200
Compare that to mobile, short, volatile bursts of activity.

8
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.600
The user expects an immediate cold start and closes the app before the engine warms up.

9
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.800
iOS also imposes a strict security constraint.

10
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.800
Apple prohibits third-party processes from executing jit compilation.

11
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.800
This forces JavaScript Core to run entirely without its primary optimization tier.

12
00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.000
It is tasked with running an agile mobile framework while stripped of the mechanisms that make it efficient.

13
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.600
Meta engineered an open source alternative named Hermes, a runtime designed specifically to resolve this mismatch for the React Native workload.

14
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.800
Hermes operates on a distinct architectural principle.

15
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.800
It moves the expensive work of compiling JavaScript completely off the user's device.

16
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.200
Relying on a web-optimized engine for a mobile framework creates a hard performance ceiling.

17
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:28.400
Breaking through that performance ceiling requires a runtime explicitly tuned to the exact demands of a mobile device.

18
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.000
This matrix compares execution models.

19
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.200
JavaScript Core interprets raw JS at runtime,

20
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.400
spending crucial milliseconds lexing and compiling on device before becoming interactive.

21
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.800
Hermes relies on pre-compiled bytecode.

22
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.000
Those exact steps happen during your CICD build, far from the user.

23
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.800
During this build step, the React Native Bundler transforms your standard JavaScript output

24
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.400
into a file formatted as Hermes bytecode or .hbc.

25
00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:00.400
This .hbc file is a compact binary representation of Hermes instruction set.

26
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.200
Structured so the engine can read it immediately without translation.

27
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:12.200
The on-device interpretation required by JavaScript Core forces the device to load a full abstract syntax tree into active memory,

28
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.400
causing a large memory spike the moment the app launches.

29
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.200
Hermes bypasses this memory spike entirely.

30
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.200
Because the bytecode is pre-compiled, the engine never constructs an abstract syntax tree on the device.

31
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.600
For native iOS developers, this mental model is identical to Swift.

32
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:32.000
Swift code is compiled to machine code during the xcode build,

33
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.000
not interpreted while the user is watching the launch screen.

34
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.200
By aligning the compilation pipelines,

35
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.800
the JavaScript runtime adopts the predictable execution characteristics of native code.

36
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.600
Time to interactive improves, not because the engine executes JavaScript faster on the device,

37
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.200
but because the device no longer has to compile the code in the first place.

38
00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:57.200
This cutaway view of the iOS.ipa archive shows the pre-compiled main.js bundle

39
00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.600
next to the embedded Hermes framework binary.

40
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.200
Embedding an engine requires physical space,

41
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.800
increasing the app store download size by roughly one and a half megabytes.

42
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:13.400
The second cost is pipeline duration, pushing the ahead of time compilation step to the server,

43
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.600
adds between five and 15 seconds to your production builds.

44
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.400
JavaScript core avoids both of these costs.

45
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.200
It adds zero bytes to the app payload and zero seconds to the build,

46
00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:28.800
because the framework is already pre-installed on the host operating system.

47
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.600
This bar chart displays the practical return on that investment.

48
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.000
A standard JavaScript core startup on mid-range devices

49
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.400
takes between 600 and 900 milliseconds.

50
00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:44.800
Hermes pulls that identical workload down to the 300 to 500 millisecond range.

51
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.000
Cutting the time to interactive in half directly impacts user retention

52
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.400
during the single most critical friction point of the application.

53
00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.200
Launch.

54
00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.600
The React Native build system handles this automatically.

55
00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.800
The use React Native CocoaPod directive resolves the dependency graph

56
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.800
and pins the exact engine artifact without manual intervention.

57
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.200
Paying a static tax of 15 seconds on a build server

58
00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:14.400
and a single megabyte of disk space is justified when it guarantees half-second cold starts for the end user.

59
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.600
Startup time is only the first phase.

60
00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:21.000
The runtime must also efficiently manage the continuous allocation and destruction

61
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.600
of volatile user interface state in memory.

62
00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.600
In Swift, Native objects are managed by automatic reference counting or ARC.

63
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.600
Memory drops deterministically the moment the last strong reference is released,

64
00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.000
requiring zero thread blocking scans.

65
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.000
This diagram shows JavaScript objects living on a cyclical Hades track,

66
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.600
separate from the linear Swift ARC track.

67
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.000
Hades operates as a generational collector,

68
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.000
dividing the short-lived UI memory into an active young generation and a slower old generation.

69
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.200
Those volatile UI objects are allocated strictly to the young generation.

70
00:04:54.200 --> 00:05:00.000
Hades sweeps this zone aggressively, collecting memory with thread pauses under one millisecond.

71
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.600
The performance risk occurs when large, long-lived data structures

72
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.400
survive multiple sweeps and migrate to the old generation,

73
00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.800
historically requiring massive thread blocking pauses.

74
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:15.200
Hades solves this through a concurrent collector with a snapshot right barrier.

75
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.400
This allows Hades to scan and clear the old generation in the background.

76
00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.800
The primary JavaScript thread keeps running,

77
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:24.200
avoiding frame drops during cleanup.

78
00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.600
Even during major collection events in a production environment,

79
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.000
Hermes restricts garbage collection pauses to under five milliseconds.

80
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.800
When these two separate memory systems need to interact,

81
00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:39.200
the turbo-mossual infrastructure coordinates the boundary via JSI calls.

82
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.200
Developers must still manage memory intentionally.

83
00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:48.800
Retaining massive data structures inside JavaScript cultures can overwhelm the throughput capacity of Hades,

84
00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.600
resulting in noticeable UI stutters.

85
00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.400
By separating these life cycles,

86
00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.200
Hermes successfully isolates the chaotic memory demands of JavaScript state

87
00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.400
from the strict execution of native interfaces.

88
00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.000
As of React Native 0.74,

89
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.200
this entire architecture is the default configuration for iOS,

90
00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.800
requiring no modifications to your pod file to enable.

91
00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.800
Despite replacing Apple's native JavaScript engine,

92
00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:19.000
developers retain the ability to profile the Hermes virtual machine natively

93
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:21.000
using Xcode instruments.

94
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.800
This is the Xcode instruments time profiler.

95
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.800
To analyze JavaScript execution,

96
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.600
developers locate the specific comm.facebook.react.javascript thread

97
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.200
or Hermes.js thread in the call tree,

98
00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.000
expanding it to reveal the runtime internals.

99
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.800
But off-device compilation introduces an operational trap.

100
00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.400
Because Hermes executes raw byte code,

101
00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:46.000
an unhandled crash in production will default to printing a stack trace

102
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.400
full of unreadable hexadecimal byte code offsets.

103
00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.200
Developers must explicitly configure their release builds

104
00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.600
to include the dash output source map compiler flag.

105
00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:58.600
This animation demonstrates what that flag produces,

106
00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.000
a .hbc.map file.

107
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.700
When an unreadable hexadecimal crash offset is fed through this map,

108
00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:07.600
it instantly translates the byte code location back into the

109
00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.700
original JavaScript function name.

110
00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:11.900
This source map artifact must be explicitly

111
00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.600
routed to your third-party crash telemetry services,

112
00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.900
like Sentry or Firebase Crashlytics during the build.

113
00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.700
The engine translates a dynamic language built for the web

114
00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.700
into a predictable ahead of time compiled asset.

115
00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.700
Hermes resolves the architectural mismatch between react native

116
00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.700
and iOS, allowing the JavaScript runtime to mirror

117
00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:32.700
the pre-compiled predictable execution model of native Swift.

